1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:04,333
We are often told to turn the heat down,

2
00:00:04,413 --> 00:00:08,681
but rarely when, by how
much, or what it saves.

3
00:00:08,761 --> 00:00:15,203
Setback works that out for one home and
gives you a schedule to set tonight.

4
00:00:16,083 --> 00:00:18,611
I click Try the Waterloo example.

5
00:00:19,491 --> 00:00:23,324
It is an illustrative
example, not a real user:

6
00:00:23,404 --> 00:00:28,977
a Waterloo apartment with a gas
furnace, at 22 degrees all day.

7
00:00:29,057 --> 00:00:35,933
The suggested schedule: 21 degrees when
home and awake, 18 when asleep or out.

8
00:00:36,013 --> 00:00:40,281
The result: simulated heating
energy falls 8.8 percent,

9
00:00:40,361 --> 00:00:45,933
about 150 kilograms of CO2e this
season, under these assumptions.

10
00:00:46,013 --> 00:00:51,585
Gas use falls from 879
to 801 cubic metres.

11
00:00:51,665 --> 00:00:57,672
That is 78 cubic metres not burned,
for one apartment, in one season.

12
00:00:58,552 --> 00:01:02,385
Comfort check: the room
is below 20 degrees

13
00:01:02,465 --> 00:01:07,603
for 6.9 of the 1,934 home-and-awake hours.

14
00:01:08,483 --> 00:01:15,359
This is the coldest week, the week of
January 26, averaging minus 15 outside.

15
00:01:15,439 --> 00:01:20,142
At night the indoor temperature
drifts down toward 18 degrees

16
00:01:20,222 --> 00:01:24,055
and the heating power
is off while it falls.

17
00:01:24,135 --> 00:01:28,403
A cooler room loses less
heat. That is the saving.

18
00:01:29,283 --> 00:01:35,290
Now the honest part. Natural Resources
Canada's rule of thumb is about 2 percent

19
00:01:35,370 --> 00:01:39,203
for a drop of 1 degree over eight hours.

20
00:01:39,283 --> 00:01:42,246
My model gives 1.4 percent.

21
00:01:42,326 --> 00:01:47,463
It lands below that, and below
a CMHC test house figure too,

22
00:01:47,543 --> 00:01:49,637
so this estimate leans low.

23
00:01:49,717 --> 00:01:53,116
I report that instead of tuning the model.

24
00:01:53,996 --> 00:01:58,263
In Scale it, I enter 40 homes like this.

25
00:01:58,343 --> 00:02:03,046
That is about 3,100 cubic metres of gas

26
00:02:03,126 --> 00:02:08,263
and about 5,990 kilograms
of CO2e per season.

27
00:02:08,343 --> 00:02:11,307
That is straight multiplication,
assuming identical homes.

28
00:02:12,187 --> 00:02:16,890
The card, Program this tonight, lists
the exact times and temperatures.

29
00:02:16,970 --> 00:02:20,803
I can print it or copy a share link.

30
00:02:20,883 --> 00:02:24,716
The link holds every
input. There is no backend.

31
00:02:25,596 --> 00:02:28,994
Sources and assumptions lists
every constant, its source,

32
00:02:29,074 --> 00:02:31,168
and which values are estimates.

33
00:02:31,248 --> 00:02:35,516
The limits: this is a simulation,
not a measured saving,

34
00:02:35,596 --> 00:02:40,733
from a single-zone model with estimated
home values and one past winter.

35
00:02:40,813 --> 00:02:47,690
I built Setback with an AI coding
agent, Claude Fable 5.2 in Claude Code,

36
00:02:47,770 --> 00:02:51,168
and the README lists
what I decided myself.
